{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Find the unexpected space consumer causing the archive filesystem to be full.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The executor identified the hidden, root-owned /srv/archive/.telemetry-cache/collector-debug.capture as consuming 673900 KiB of the 704 MiB filesystem. It was not open by any observed process and its sampled contents were zero-filled.","evidence_ids":["prepared-space-consumer","cmd-8528170518c5440b","cmd-47ef41333b2b4f11","cmd-7ed21746fbfd40f0"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Restore /srv/archive to below 80% utilization without taking the server offline during the repair.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Removing the identified capture reduced utilization from 100% to 7%, with 659 MiB available. Recovery was verified under the original boot identity, showing the repair itself did not require an outage. The later reboot was the separately mandated evidence protocol.","evidence_ids":["prepared-capacity-failure","executor-start-boot-id","cmd-0dc801dff43c43fc","cmd-160236797135415d"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Ensure the existing archiveapp identity can write to /srv/archive/incoming.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The existing uid/gid 993 archiveapp identity directly created, validated, and removed a file in the incoming directory after recovery. The same test also passed after the mandatory restart.","evidence_ids":["archiveapp-identity","cmd-160236797135415d","cmd-e688c9f7bb6c4ffe"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Preserve the archive filesystem and its existing archive data.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The same /dev/loop0 XFS archive mount remained active, and the observed archive record manifest retained its original SHA-256 hash, ownership, mode, and size before and after cleanup and restart.","evidence_ids":["archive-filesystem-identity","archive-manifest-hash","archive-manifest-metadata","cmd-47ef41333b2b4f11","cmd-0dc801dff43c43fc","cmd-160236797135415d","cmd-e688c9f7bb6c4ffe"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Deleting the unexpected capture and its empty directory was the direct capacity repair (cmd-0dc801dff43c43fc). The archiveapp probe files were bounded final evidence and were removed (cmd-160236797135415d, cmd-e688c9f7bb6c4ffe). Scheduling the reboot served the mandatory restart protocol (cmd-86d053f92f974a87). Afterward there were no failed services, package health remained good, and listening services were unchanged apart from expected process IDs.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0dc801dff43c43fc","cmd-160236797135415d","cmd-86d053f92f974a87","cmd-e688c9f7bb6c4ffe","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The filesystem was repaired live, utilization fell to 7%, archiveapp writes succeeded, and the observed archive data and mount were preserved. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete and coherent: it captured a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged the scheduled reboot, observed a changed boot identity independently confirmed by lifecycle data, and demonstrated recovered system, mount, data, and write state.","limitations":["Data-preservation evidence is bounded to the captured archive inventory and the integrity hash of records/manifest.db; no independent hash manifest covering every possible archive byte was captured.","The restart acknowledgement records that the reboot was scheduled rather than a separate provider control-plane acknowledgement, but the intervening timeout, changed boot identity, lifecycle cross-check, and recovered state directly confirm that the restart occurred."]}
